```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies entirely on router0, not on router1's WAN uplink. Router0's FRR
RIP daemon (ripd) is not running and its FRR configuration is completely empty —
there is no "router rip" block whatsoever. As a result, router0 participates in
no dynamic routing protocol and its kernel forwarding table contains only its
three directly-connected prefixes: 192.168.1.0/24 (CS, eth0), 192.168.2.0/24
(IT, eth1), and 10.0.0.0/8 (WAN, eth2). It has no routes to 192.168.3.0/24
(Office) or 192.168.4.0/24 (Principle).

The mechanism is an asymmetric black-hole: the forward path from Office/Principle
hosts to CS/IT hosts actually works end-to-end — router1 holds valid RIP-learned
routes (192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.2.0/24 via 10.10.0.1/eth2) and forwards
correctly to router0, which delivers packets to the CS/IT LANs. However, when
CS/IT hosts attempt to reply, they send the return packet to router0 (their
default gateway). Router0 looks up the source address (192.168.3.x or
192.168.4.x), finds no matching route, and silently drops the reply. This
produces the observed timeout behaviour — requests go out, but no ICMP echo
replies ever return.

Router1's eth2 (wan_r0_r1, 10.10.0.2/8) is physically UP, correctly addressed,
and its RIP daemon is running and advertising all four subnets — there is nothing
wrong with router1 or its WAN uplink.

Root cause: router0's FRR configuration was never provisioned with a RIP routing
block, so ripd was never started and router0 has no routes to the Office or
Principle subnets.

Fix: add a "router rip" stanza to router0's FRR configuration that enables RIP
version 2 and includes all four directly-attached networks (192.168.1.0/24,
192.168.2.0/24, 10.0.0.0/8, and if needed a loopback/management network), then
reload FRR on router0. Once ripd starts, it will exchange updates with router1
over the 10.10.0.0/8 WAN link, install routes to 192.168.3.0/24 and
192.168.4.0/24, and bidirectional connectivity will be restored.
=== END ===
```